    "message": "perf(request-log): reject non-matching bodies before decoding them\n\nSearch over compressed bodies decompressed and JSON-parsed every row,\nwhich made a selective search 6.3x slower than the inline form -- 9\nseconds against 1.4 on 4,000 real requests.\n\nMost rows do not match, and for those the parse and the UTF-8 decode are\npure waste. JSON encoding only rewrites quotes, backslashes and control\ncharacters, so a needle containing none of them survives into the stored\nblob byte for byte: absent from the encoded bytes proves absent from the\ndecoded text. That makes a bytes-level test a sound rejection filter.\n\nIt is not sound in the other direction -- it can match a key name or the\nstructural punctuation -- so a hit is still verified against the decoded\ntext, and a needle that JSON would escape skips the filter entirely.\n\nSelective search 9,056ms -> 1,919ms, now within 1.3x of inline. A term\nmatching almost every row is unchanged, because then the decode is the\nwork rather than the waste.\n\nTests pin the parts that could go quietly wrong: needles containing\nquotes, backslashes and newlines still match; structural fragments like\n'\",\"' do not; and inline and compressed stores return the same rows for\nthe same searches.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
